.TH CHTTPSVR_RESP_SET_STATUS 3 "2026-07-10" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
chttpsvr_resp_set_status \- set the HTTP response status code
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/chttpserver.h>

void chttpsvr_resp_set_status(chttpsvr_resp *resp, int status_code);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Sets the HTTP status code of the response.  The default is 200.

The value is stored exactly as given, with no check here.  When the
server sends the response, a value outside the range of final status
codes, 200 to 999, goes on the wire as 500 Internal Server Error,
although the stored value stays what you set.  A 1xx falls in that
case too: it is an interim status (RFC 9110 15.2), and a client that
got one as the response would keep waiting for a final status.  The
server writes the interim "100 Continue" itself.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
With a status_code of 204, 205 or 304 the response body is dropped on
the wire, including anything that chttpsvr_resp_write,
chttpsvr_resp_write_str, chttpsvr_resp_printf or
chttpsvr_resp_write_json has already written, since RFC 9110 6.4.1,
15.3.6 and 15.4.5 forbid a body for these codes.  A 205 carries
"content-length:0", a 204 carries no Content-Length, and a 304
carries the one that the handler set, if it set one.  See the NOTES
section of chttpsvr_resp_write(3).

The status line carries the reason phrase that the IANA HTTP Status
Code Registry gives the code, such as "Reset Content" for 205, and
"Unknown" for a code the registry does not list.  Clients ignore the
phrase.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR chttpsvr_resp_set_header (3),
.BR chttpsvr_resp_write (3)
